# Public page SEO

When creating or editing a **public** (indexable) page, every page MUST ship:

1. **`<title>`** — unique, human-readable, roughly 50–60 characters
2. **meta description** — unique summary, roughly 120–160 characters
3. **canonical** — absolute URL via `<link rel="canonical" href="…">` (or framework equivalent)
4. **robots** — project-level `robots.txt` (or route) that allows crawling of public URLs and points at the sitemap
5. **sitemap** — `sitemap.xml` (or generated sitemap) listing all public URLs

Private / auth-only / preview pages MUST set `noindex` (and usually `nofollow`) instead of a public canonical-to-index path.

## Prefer framework APIs

Use the project's metadata API when it exists (e.g. Next.js `metadata` / `generateMetadata`, Remix `meta`, Astro `<head>`). Do not invent a second head system.

```ts
// ✅ Next.js App Router example
export const metadata = {
  title: "Pricing",
  description: "Simple plans for teams that ship.",
  alternates: { canonical: "https://example.com/pricing" },
  robots: { index: true, follow: true },
};
```

```html
<!-- ✅ Plain HTML -->
<title>Pricing</title>
<meta name="description" content="Simple plans for teams that ship." />
<link rel="canonical" href="https://example.com/pricing" />
```

## robots + sitemap (once per site)

- `robots.txt` MUST include `Sitemap: https://<host>/sitemap.xml` (or the real sitemap URL).
- Sitemap MUST list only public, canonical URLs (no `noindex`, no duplicates, prefer HTTPS).
- When adding a public page, update the sitemap (or the generator that builds it) in the same change.

## Checklist before done

- [ ] Unique title
- [ ] Unique description
- [ ] Absolute canonical
- [ ] Public → indexable; private → `noindex`
- [ ] `robots.txt` present and references sitemap
- [ ] New public URL included in sitemap
